.\" Man page for nic-health.sh
.\" Written in classic troff/groff format
.TH NIC-HEALTH 8 "2026-10-26" "nic-health 2.13.0" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
nic-health \- physical NIC hardware and data-path health checks with a monitoring verdict
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nic-health.sh
.RI [ \-i | \-\-interface
.IR IFACE ]...
.RI [ \-I | \-\-interval
.IR SEC ]
.RI [ \-L | \-\-lookback
.IR MIN ]
.RI [ \-O | \-\-output
.IR table | csv | json ]
.RI [ \-l | \-\-level
.IR basic | deep ]
.RI [ \-a | \-\-all ]
.RI [ \-n | \-\-no\-color ]
.RI [ \-t | \-\-check\-tools ]
.RI [ \-v | \-\-version ]
.RI [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nic-health.sh
is the companion of
.BR nic-xray.sh (8).
Where nic-xray answers "what is installed and how is it cabled",
nic-health answers "is the NIC hardware and its data path healthy".
It evaluates a set of checks and reports each result with a severity
.RB ( OK ", " WARN ", " CRIT ", " UNKNOWN ", " N/A ),
the measured value, the threshold, the evidence it was derived from, and a
remediation hint. The overall verdict is returned as a Nagios-compatible exit
code, so the script can be used directly as a monitoring plugin.
.PP
Ports of the same physical card (ASIC: PCI domain, bus and device) are grouped;
card-level checks run once per ASIC, port-level checks once per interface.
Non-PCI interfaces (for example a BMC USB network interface) are listed as
.B N/A
and not evaluated.
.PP
.B Read-only.
The default
.B basic
level only reads sysfs, procfs,
.BR ethtool (8)
and
.BR devlink (8)
state. The
.B deep
level additionally runs one allowlisted query per port with a vendor tool (see
.BR "DEEP MODE" ).
No level ever changes NIC, driver, link or firmware state.
.PP
.B Counter windows.
Cumulative counters are read twice,
.I SEC
seconds apart (default 5), and only growth inside that window is judged.
Historical totals since boot are reported as context, never as a current fault.
A counter that decreases (reset), appears or disappears during the window is
reported as
.BR UNKNOWN ,
never as
.BR OK .
With
.BR "\-I 0" ,
a single read of totals since boot or driver load is evaluated instead; those
results are historical, so severity is capped at
.BR WARN .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interface " \fIIFACE\fP"
Evaluate only
.IR IFACE .
Repeatable. Default: every physical NIC (loopback, bridges, bonds, tap/tun,
VLAN sub-interfaces and similar virtual devices are skipped). An unknown or
virtual interface exits with status 3.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-interval " \fISEC\fP"
Counter sampling window in seconds (0\-3600, default 5).
.B 0
selects absolute mode (totals since boot or driver load, severity capped at
.BR WARN ).
The default suits quick sweeps and monitoring. For troubleshooting use
.B "\-I 30"
or longer: bursty link errors can fall outside a short window, so a bad link
can look clean in one run and fail the next (the history floor of
.B mlx5.phy.errors
and
.B mlx5.frame.errors
keeps such a link at WARN or worse).
In table output a progress bar is shown on standard error while sampling.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-lookback " \fIMIN\fP"
Count driver link-down messages in the kernel journal over the last
.I MIN
minutes (0\-1440, default 60;
.B 0
turns the look-back off). One read-only
.B "journalctl \-k"
query per run, bounded by
.BR timeout (1)
when available. Only the current boot is searched, the same lifetime as the
.I carrier_down_count
shown as context. See
.BR generic.link.lookback .
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-output " \fIFORMAT\fP"
Output format:
.B table
(default),
.BR csv ,
or
.BR json .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-level " \fILEVEL\fP"
.B basic
(default): passive reads only.
.BR deep :
also query each mlx5 physical function with
.BR mlxlink (1)
or
.BR mstlink (1).
Requires
.BR timeout (1).
See
.BR "DEEP MODE" .
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Table output: also list
.B OK
and
.B N/A
rows. By default only
.BR WARN ,
.B CRIT
and
.B UNKNOWN
rows are listed, followed by a count of hidden rows; a host without problems
prints a single line. CSV and JSON always contain every result.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-color
Disable ANSI colors. Colors are disabled automatically when standard output is
not a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-check\-tools
List the required and optional tools, whether each was found, what it enables,
and which package provides it, then exit. Does not require root.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print
.B nic-health.sh
and the version, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage, options, the check list and exit codes, then exit.
.SH CHECKS
Every result carries a check ID. Generic checks apply to all drivers; vendor
checks only to the matching driver.
.SS Generic (all drivers)
.TP
.B generic.pcie.link
ASIC trained below what both ends of the link support (speed or width) \(-> WARN.
The expected link is the lower of the card's maxima and those of the PCIe port
above it (slot or bridge), so a card wired to a narrower or slower slot by
design (for example an on-board x2 chip on an x1 port) is OK, with the slot
limit in the evidence and a "(slot)" threshold. A slot reduced by a BIOS
bifurcation or link-speed setting looks the same and is also OK; the hint
says to check the BIOS. Without upstream port link attributes the card's
maxima are used.
No PCIe link attributes (virtual function, chipset-integrated NIC) \(-> N/A.
.TP
.B generic.pcie.aer
PCIe Advanced Error Reporting, summed over the ASIC's functions.
New fatal or non-fatal errors in the window \(-> CRIT; new correctable errors \(-> WARN.
.TP
.B generic.temperature
Each hwmon temperature sensor of the ASIC (deduplicated by label). At or above
the critical threshold \(-> CRIT. Within 10 \(deC of critical, above a driver
maximum that is lower than critical, or with the alarm flag set \(-> WARN.
A sensor the firmware lists but cannot read (EBUSY) \(-> N/A.
A critical or maximum threshold at or below 0 \(deC is ignored (mlx5 reports
input 0, critical 0 for an empty module cage); a sensor left with no threshold
and no alarm \(-> N/A.
.TP
.B generic.devlink.health.\fIREPORTER\fP
devlink health reporters, device level (target: PCI function) and port level
(target: interface). State other than healthy \(-> CRIT; new errors in the
window \(-> WARN. No reporters for the ASIC, or devlink not installed \(-> N/A.
.TP
.B generic.link.state
A port without carrier that is in use \(-> WARN; CRIT when its bond is down
too (all members lost); N/A when the operator downed the bond. In use means a
bond, bridge, team or OVS master, an upper device (VLAN, macvlan), SR-IOV
virtual functions, or a global IP address (needs
.BR ip (8)).
A spare port without link, or an administratively down port, is N/A: not a
fault. Admin state alone cannot tell them apart, because provisioning usually
brings every port up. The check never calls a port spare when it cannot be
sure: if
.BR ip (8)
is missing or fails the result is UNKNOWN, and a port whose link came up more
than once this boot (spare ports show a single up/down pair from driver
start) is WARN. Carrier up with operstate dormant, lowerlayerdown or testing
(for example 802.1X not yet authorized) \(-> WARN. Ports used only outside the
host's view (DPDK, PCI passthrough without VFs) cannot be recognized; they are
judged as spare unless they had link this boot. A team master that is down
does not escalate to CRIT.
.TP
.B generic.link.flaps
Carrier transitions in the window \(-> WARN.
.TP
.B generic.link.lookback
Driver link-down messages for the port in the kernel journal over the last
.B \-L
minutes. One or more \(-> WARN; 3 or more while the port is also flapping in
the counter window \(-> CRIT. History alone never goes beyond WARN.
A line counts when the interface name is a word of its own followed by a
link-down phrase (mlx5 "Link down", ice/i40e/ixgbe/igb/bnxt_en "NIC Link is
Down", tg3 "Link is down."), so bond and bridge messages about the same event
are not counted twice. The kernel rate-limits repeated messages, so the count
is a lower bound. Lines logged before an interface was renamed carry the old
name: they are not counted, or are attributed to whichever port now has that
name. A journal that keeps less than the window (small or volatile journal,
.I ReadKMSG=no
in
.BR journald.conf (5))
yields a lower count, not an error. Without
.BR journalctl (1),
or with no kernel journal for the current boot (containers, journald
disabled), a single host row reports N/A; a failed or timed-out query reports
UNKNOWN. Never reported as 0 when the journal could not be read.
.SS mlx5_core (NVIDIA ConnectX), from ethtool \-S
Evaluated on physical functions only. Each rule lists counters whose growth
escalates to WARN or CRIT, and context counters that are only shown.
.TP
.B mlx5.rx.starvation
rx_out_of_buffer, rx_buff_alloc_err \(-> WARN (host cannot drain RX rings).
.TP
.B mlx5.nic.discards
rx_discards_phy, tx_discards_phy \(-> WARN (NIC buffer exhausted).
.TP
.B mlx5.phy.errors
rx_symbol_err_phy, rx_pcs_symbol_err_phy \(-> CRIT (uncorrectable symbol errors,
traffic is lost). Pre-FEC BER above 1E-06 \(-> WARN. The BER uses rx_bits_phy
when the card exposes it; otherwise (ConnectX-6 Lx) it is estimated from the
link speed and the window length, shown as "pre-FEC BER~", and judged only
when every lane runs at 25 Gb/s or less (NRZ; PAM4 lanes run a higher pre-FEC
BER by design). Adds a lane-skew hint when one lane carries most corrected bits,
counting only the lanes the link uses (ethtool "Lanes:", else one lane at
25 Gb/s or less), and a FEC hint when the link runs at 25 Gb/s or more with FEC off.
.TP
.B mlx5.frame.errors
CRC, fragment, jabber, undersize and oversize frames \(-> WARN.
.IP
History floor (both rules above, delta mode only): when none of the escalating
counters grow in the window, a counter whose total since boot is at least 1000
and averages at least 60 an hour over the host uptime \(-> WARN, with the value
"history: COUNTER ~N/h". The totals only reset at driver reload or reboot, so a
link cleaned after an incident stays at WARN until the average falls below the
floor; watch it with a longer window to see whether errors still grow. Uptime is
at least the time since driver load, so the average is a lower bound. History
alone never gives CRIT.
.TP
.B mlx5.pcie.path
PCIe signal-integrity recoveries and outbound stall events \(-> WARN; stall
percentage gauges shown as context.
.TP
.B mlx5.datapath.faults
tx_cqe_err, rx_wqe_err, tx_recover, rx_recover \(-> CRIT.
.TP
.B mlx5.congestion
Per-priority buffer and congestion discards \(-> WARN. Pause frames and buffer
threshold crossings are context only: congestion is not a NIC fault.
.TP
.B mlx5.pause.storm
Pause-storm warning \(-> WARN, pause-storm error \(-> CRIT.
.TP
.B mlx5.module
module_bus_stuck, module_bad_shorted \(-> CRIT; module_high_temp,
module_unplug \(-> WARN.
.TP
.B mlx5.link.phy_down
link_down_events_phy \(-> WARN.
.TP
.B mlx5.ethtool
ethtool \-S failed for the port \(-> UNKNOWN.
.SS Deep mode (\-l deep, mlx5_core)
.TP
.B mlx5.deep.troubleshooting
The tool's Status Opcode is not 0 \(-> WARN, with the tool's recommendation.
.TP
.B mlx5.deep.fec
The module is specified for operation with FEC but the link runs without FEC
\(-> WARN.
.TP
.B mlx5.deep.ber
Effective (post-FEC) BER of 1E-12 or more \(-> WARN. This is an average since the
counters were last cleared, so it never escalates to CRIT. The evidence lists
the effective and raw BER and error counts, plus the per-lane raw errors and
link-down counter when the tool prints them (mstflint 4.26 has no link-down
counter; MFT and newer mstflint do).
.TP
.B mlx5.deep.module
Module temperature, voltage, bias current or optical power outside the module's
own range, or any lane with Tx fault, Rx LOS or CDR loss of lock \(-> WARN.
Multi-lane modules (for example QSFP28 100G SR4) are judged lane by lane: the
value shows the lowest and highest lane, the evidence lists every lane and the
hint names the lane at fault.
.TP
.B mlx5.deep
The query failed or timed out \(-> UNKNOWN. No deep tool installed \(-> N/A.
.SH SEVERITIES
.TP
.B OK
Checked and within thresholds.
.TP
.B WARN
Degraded, or a historical problem that should be looked at.
.TP
.B CRIT
Active fault: traffic loss, uncorrectable errors, unhealthy firmware reporter.
.TP
.B UNKNOWN
The check could not produce a trustworthy result (unreadable source, counter
reset or disappearance during the window, tool failure). Never reported as OK.
.TP
.B N/A
Not applicable to this hardware or driver, or the data source does not exist.
.SH DEEP MODE
Deep mode is an explicit consent boundary and only runs with
.BR "\-l deep" .
For each mlx5 physical function exactly one command is run:
.PP
.RS
.B timeout \-k 2 10 mlxlink \-d
.I BDF
.B \-m \-c
.RE
.PP
.BR mlxlink (1)
(NVIDIA MFT) is preferred;
.BR mstlink (1)
(open-source mstflint package) is the fallback. Before every call an allowlist
gate checks the tool name, that the argument list matches this template exactly,
that the PCI address is well formed, and that no forbidden flag is present;
refused calls are reported as UNKNOWN and never executed. Tools are executed by
absolute path, and shell functions inherited from the environment are discarded
at start-up.
.PP
On a port without carrier the tool can only report the missing link, so
.BR mlx5.deep.troubleshooting ,
.B mlx5.deep.fec
and
.B mlx5.deep.ber
are N/A there (the link itself is judged by
.BR generic.link.state ),
and
.B mlx5.deep.module
shows Rx power, Rx LOS and Rx CDR LOL without judging them, since no light is
expected; temperature, voltage, bias, Tx power and Tx fault flags are still
judged.
.PP
Never run, at any level: eye scans, PRBS, link/FEC/speed changes, counter
clears, error injection, register access
.RB ( mlxreg ", " mstreg ),
firmware operations
.RB ( mlxfwmanager ", " mstflint ", " mstfwreset ),
.BR "mst start" ,
devlink health dump/recover/reload,
.BR "ethtool \-t" / \-r ,
and unbounded kernel log reads.
.B mlxconfig
and
.B mstconfig
queries are not used: they take several seconds of kernel time per device and
report configuration rather than health.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
.SS Table (default)
A header with host, collection time (UTC), sampling window and look-back; a hardware
summary (ASIC, model, driver, firmware, ports); the result rows (see
.BR \-a );
and a summary line with the count per severity and the verdict.
.SS CSV
One row per result with columns: severity, scope, target, check_id, value,
unit, threshold, evidence, hint, ref. Fields are quoted (RFC 4180).
.SS JSON
An object with schema_version, tool, version, host, collected_at, uptime_s
(host uptime in seconds at collected_at), boot_id (kernel boot ID, changes on
every reboot; both null if unreadable), level,
lookback_min (0 when the look-back is off), mode
.RB ( delta " or " absolute ),
interval_s, elapsed_s (null if the clock was unreadable), capabilities (tool
availability), summary (count per severity), verdict, exit_code, asics (model,
driver, firmware, ports) and results (one object per result, same fields as
CSV).
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
Bash 4.4 or newer.
.IP \(bu 2
Root privileges (except for
.BR \-\-help ,
.B \-\-version
and
.BR \-\-check\-tools ).
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ethtool (8),
.BR getopt (1)
(util-linux),
.BR readlink (1)
(coreutils).
.SH RECOMMENDATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR lspci (8)
(pciutils): vendor and model names; without it raw PCI IDs are shown.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR devlink (8)
(iproute2): devlink health reporter checks.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR timeout (1)
(coreutils): required for
.BR "\-l deep" .
.IP \(bu 2
mstflint (distribution package) or NVIDIA MFT: deep mode on ConnectX cards.
MFT is proprietary and must be installed manually; it is never bundled or
downloaded. Installing it with
.B "install.sh \-\-without\-kernel"
is enough:
.B mlxlink
reaches the card by PCI address without the MFT kernel module.
.IP \(bu 2
bash-completion: tab completion via
.IR completions/nic\-health.bash .
Install system-wide with
.BR "make install\-completion" ,
or load it in the current shell with
.BR "source completions/nic\-health.bash" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
Nagios-compatible; precedence CRIT > WARN > UNKNOWN > OK.
.TP
.B 0
All results OK or N/A.
.TP
.B 1
At least one WARN and no CRIT.
.TP
.B 2
At least one CRIT.
.TP
.B 3
UNKNOWN: usage error, not run as root, missing required tool, no eligible NIC,
or UNKNOWN results without any WARN or CRIT.
.SH EXAMPLES
Evaluate every physical NIC with a 5-second window, problems only:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo nic-health.sh
.RE
.PP
List every result, including OK and N/A:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo nic-health.sh \-a
.RE
.PP
Two interfaces, 30-second window (recommended when troubleshooting a link):
.PP
.RS
.B sudo nic-health.sh \-i ens1f0np0 \-i ens1f1np1 \-I 30
.RE
.PP
Historical totals since boot (no waiting; severity capped at WARN):
.PP
.RS
.B sudo nic-health.sh \-I 0
.RE
.PP
Link-downs over the last 24 hours (for example after an overnight incident):
.PP
.RS
.B sudo nic-health.sh \-L 1440
.RE
.PP
Include allowlisted vendor tool queries on ConnectX cards:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo nic-health.sh \-l deep
.RE
.PP
JSON for a monitoring system or for later comparison:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo nic-health.sh \-O json > nic-health.json
.RE
.PP
Show which tools are present and what each enables:
.PP
.RS
.B nic-health.sh \-\-check\-tools
.RE
